# Disabling PHP Execution for HTML Styling Without Breaking Logic

You want to style your HTML freely, but you're running into a wall where PHP code is being executed before the browser can render your CSS or layout. This is a common friction point when building hybrid applications that mix server-side logic with client-side markup. The goal isn't to "disable" PHP entirely for the file, but rather to control *when* it executes so you can see your raw HTML structure while keeping your application logic intact.

### Why `<?php` Tags Don't Just Disappear

The core misunderstanding often lies in how PHP tags work. When a server processes a `.php` file, it looks for `<?php ... ?>` blocks. Anything outside these blocks is treated as literal text to be sent to the browser. However, if your file starts with `<?php`, every single line of code inside that block executes before the HTML is ever seen by the client.

You cannot simply "comment out" PHP code and expect it to stop executing logic while still allowing you to style the surrounding HTML. If you use `//` or `/* */` inside a PHP block, the code is skipped, but the *execution context* remains active until the closing `?>`. If your file starts with PHP, you cannot see the HTML structure because the server hasn't finished parsing the logic yet.

### The Solution: Move Logic to Included Files

The standard pattern in PHP developmentâespecially in frameworks like Laravel or simple MVC setupsâis to separate concerns. You should move all executable logic (database connections, session handling, input sanitization) into a separate file and include it at the very top of your main page.

Here is how you restructure `index.php`:

```php
<?php
// 1. Include your application logic here
require_once __DIR__ . '/config.php'; 
require_once __DIR__ . '/functions.php';
// Or if using a specific file like in your source:
// require_once './../Code of Conduct/ar_uuid/ar_uuid.php';

// Check session or load data *once* before output starts
$userData = getCurrentUserData(); // Call your logic function here

?>
<!doctype html>
<html lang="sv-SE">
